          "distinct": true,
          "id": "60b9b11ba61138e3abfd1295eca73ed243d0b25d",
          "message": "docs: fix demo tape to replay the capture, not a live cluster (#127)\n\n* docs: fix demo tape to replay the capture, not a live cluster\n\nThe demo tape pointed kubectl at a real KinD cluster instead of the mock\nserver that `kshrk open` starts. It selected the kubeconfig with\n`ls ~/.kube/k8shark-*.yaml | tail -1`, but that glob also matches the dev\nand scale cluster kubeconfigs (`k8shark-dev.yaml`, `k8shark-scale.yaml`),\nand `tail -1` sorts them after the random `k8shark-<uuid>.yaml` the mock\nserver writes. So the kubectl half of the demo hit the live `k8shark-scale`\ncluster (v1.35.0, no `k8shark-test` namespace) — contradicting the capture\nshown just above (k8shark-dev, v1.36.1) and even printing \"No resources\nfound in k8shark-test namespace\".\n\nPoint `kshrk open` at a deterministic kubeconfig path via --kubeconfig-out\nand export exactly that, so kubectl always talks to the replayed capture.\nRegenerated demo.gif now shows consistent output throughout: the captured\nk8shark-dev node and the k8shark-test workloads served by the mock API.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: wait for mock kubeconfig before export in demo tape\n\nAddress review feedback: the tape exported KUBECONFIG relying only on a\nfixed Sleep after the backgrounded `kshrk open &`, which could race the\nserver on slower machines. Block until the kubeconfig is written\n(`until [ -s ... ]`) before exporting it, on a single line so the demo\nstays clean.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-26T15:29:19-04:00",
