          "distinct": true,
          "id": "52c71a101df1014820ffb6f397d7c9ae80c59b69",
          "message": "feat(server): protobuf response encoding for a uniform apiserver surface (#155)\n\n* feat(server): protobuf response encoding for a uniform apiserver surface\n\nclient-go/kubectl default to protobuf for built-in types. The mock builds all\nresponses as JSON — which protobuf clients accept, so reads already worked, but\na real apiserver replies with protobuf when asked. Match that: negotiate the\nresponse encoding.\n\nWhen a (non-watch) request's Accept prefers application/vnd.kubernetes.protobuf,\nbuffer the response and re-encode a JSON body of a scheme-known built-in type as\nprotobuf (reverse of the request-side decode from #148, reusing the same\nscheme + serializers). Bodies that aren't built-in Kubernetes objects —\nCRDs/unstructured, OpenAPI docs, health text — fail the scheme decode and pass\nthrough as JSON, exactly as a real apiserver does (CRDs have no protobuf). Watch\nstreams are never buffered.\n\nCovers GET/LIST, single-object GET, write responses, and Status/error bodies.\nVerified against a running server: protobuf Accept yields k8s\\x00-framed bodies\nthat decode back to typed objects (e.g. PodList); JSON clients and non-object\npaths (/openapi/v2) stay JSON. Tests + full `go test -race ./...`, vet, gofmt\nclean.\n\nWatch-stream protobuf framing (application/vnd.kubernetes.protobuf;stream=watch)\nremains a follow-up; watch already works over JSON.\n\nCloses #150\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(server): q-value-aware protobuf Accept negotiation\n\nwantsProtobuf parsed the Accept header with strings.Contains, which ignored\nq-values — it would pick protobuf even when a client refused it (q=0) or\npreferred JSON via a higher q. Parse media types and q-values (mime), and\nreturn true only when Kubernetes protobuf is acceptable (q>0) and at least as\npreferred as any JSON/wildcard alternative. Table requests and json-preferred\nclients now correctly get JSON.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(server): protobuf negotiation — header order, Vary, cover compat shims\n\nAddress review on the response negotiation:\n- wantsProtobuf now mirrors apiserver negotiation: highest q wins, header order\n  breaks ties. `json,protobuf` (equal q) → JSON; `protobuf,json` → protobuf.\n- Set `Vary: Accept` on negotiated responses so caches/intermediaries don't\n  reuse a protobuf response for a JSON client (or vice versa).\n- Install the response wrapper before the early POST/compat shims\n  (SelfSubject*Review, exec/proxy/replay-control), so their Status/object\n  responses are negotiated too — previously they always returned JSON.\n\nTests: header-order tie-breaker + Table-first cases; verified a\nSelfSubjectAccessReview POST with Accept protobuf now returns protobuf.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* perf(server): don't buffer non-transcodable endpoints for protobuf\n\nThe response wrapper buffered every non-watch response when the client\npreferred protobuf, including endpoints that can never be a Kubernetes\nprotobuf object and may be large or streamed — OpenAPI documents (multi-MB\nJSON) and pod log subresources (text/plain). Skip wrapping those\n(isNonProtobufPath) so they aren't buffered in memory only to pass through\nunchanged, avoiding needless latency/memory and preserving streaming.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>",
