          "id": "1857438cc218a9f5d15d4a0ec6c90efb46bd77e4",
          "message": "feat(replay-overlay): watch feedback — live overlay events + overlay-wins suppression (#158)\n\n* feat(replay-overlay): watch feedback — live overlay events + overlay-wins suppression\n\nPhase-4 PR 2 of the writable overlay (#123). Until now, overlay writes made\nduring an active watch were invisible to that watch: streamReplayWatch only\nreplayed captured-timeline events. A client-go controller (e.g. KWOK) pointed at\n`kshrk replay --writable` could not observe its own — or another client's —\nwrites, so no closed loop was possible.\n\nThis adds:\n\n- Overlay pub/sub: store/del/cascadeDeleteNamespace publish typed watch events\n  (ADDED for a new-or-recreated identity, MODIFIED for a live update, DELETED for\n  a tombstone) to subscribers scoped by GVR + namespace (namespace \"\" matches all,\n  for cluster-wide/-A watches).\n\n- Stream integration: each streamReplayWatch subscribes before the initial burst,\n  then a pump goroutine emits live overlay events for the life of the stream.\n  Frame writes from the pump and the main loop are serialized by a mutex; the\n  handler waits for the pump to stop before returning (so it never writes to a\n  dead ResponseWriter). Events already reflected in the burst are deduped by RV.\n  On buffer overflow the stream is torn down so the client relists, mirroring an\n  apiserver watch-cache overflow.\n\n- Overlay-wins suppression: replayed captured events for an identity the overlay\n  owns are dropped, so a client never sees a stale captured event after a write\n  takes ownership. (The initial/relist bursts already merge the overlay, so only\n  streamed events need this.)\n\nVerified with a real client-go dynamic informer (TestOverlay_WatchFeedback_Informer)\nobserving an overlay create + delete live — the KWOK-shaped acceptance test — plus\nlower-level live-write and suppression tests. Builds coherently with the existing\nRV/resume semantics (overlay RVs already share the resume window calc).\n\nNote: the informer test pulls the standard client-go openapi transitive deps into\ngo.mod as indirect (test-only) requirements.\n\nPart of #123.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(replay-overlay): tear down watch immediately on subscriber overflow\n\nAddress Copilot review on #158: the overflow flag was only checked when the pump\nreceived an event, so if the buffer overflowed and no further overlay write\noccurred, the pump could drain the backlog and block on the channel without ever\ntearing the stream down. Replace the atomic flag with an overflowCh that\npublishLocked closes (once) on overflow; the pump selects on it directly, so a\nrelist is forced immediately regardless of further traffic.\n\nAdds TestOverlay_SubscribeOverflow.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(replay-overlay): handle DeletedFinalStateUnknown in informer test\n\nAddress Copilot review on #158: an informer's DeleteFunc can receive a\ncache.DeletedFinalStateUnknown tombstone (e.g. after a relist, which the overflow\npath can now trigger) rather than the object directly. Unwrap it so the deletion\nis always observed and the test can't time out spuriously.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(replay-overlay): derive watch skip-RV atomically from the burst snapshot\n\nAddress Copilot review on #158: overlaySkip was read via scopeRV() at subscribe\ntime, before the burst was resolved. A write landing between subscribe and the\nburst snapshot could be both included in the burst and re-emitted by the pump.\n\nCapture the skip floor inside the burst's overlay snapshot instead: applyToList\nnow returns the highest overlay RV it merged (under the same RLock as the\nsnapshot), threaded through mergeOverlayList and resolveWatchList into the pump's\noverlaySkip. Because the overlay RV counter is monotonic, every write after the\nsnapshot has a higher RV, so it is still delivered — no duplicate and no gap. (A\nskip derived after the snapshot instead would risk dropping a post-snapshot event\nentirely, i.e. a stale watch, which is worse.)\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>",
