          "distinct": true,
          "id": "9ca0dc77ec7936914044936dea6ae330e30af355",
          "message": "feat(replay-overlay): pod scheduling shim for KWOK integration (#162)\n\n* feat(replay-overlay): pod scheduling shim for KWOK integration\n\nImplements the scheduling shim from #160. In writable replay, a Pod a client\ncreates never leaves Pending — replay has no scheduler to assign spec.nodeName,\nand KWOK's \"Pod → Running\" stage only fires once a Pod is bound to a node.\n\nOn create, the overlay now binds an unscheduled Pod to a node:\n- round-robin over the known nodes (captured + overlay),\n- synthesizing a node (kwok-node-0, annotated `kwok.x-k8s.io/node: fake` so a\n  stock `kwok` run manages it) when the capture has none,\n- leaving a Pod that already sets spec.nodeName alone.\n\nThe shim only assigns nodeName; it does not set Pod status — that stays KWOK's\njob, which keeps the loop realistic. On by default under --writable (gated by a\nhandler flag so the existing pod-create tests are unaffected); production enables\nit in serve().\n\nAdds docs/kwok.md (closed-loop walkthrough using KWOK's built-in default stages)\nand links it from usage.md. Tests cover synthesis, captured-node use, round-robin,\nexplicit-nodeName passthrough, and off-by-default.\n\nPart of #160.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(replay-overlay): take scheduling round-robin modulo in int64\n\nAddress Copilot review on #162: int(nextScheduleIndex()) % len(nodes) casts the\nint64 counter to int before the modulo, which can overflow to a negative index on\na 32-bit platform (or past math.MaxInt). Take the modulo in int64 and convert\nonly the bounded [0,len) result.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(replay-overlay): build synthetic node JSON via json.Marshal\n\nAddress Copilot review on #162: syntheticNodeBase built JSON by string\nconcatenation with the node name injected directly. The only call site passes a\nconstant, but marshaling a map escapes the name correctly and makes the helper\nsafe to reuse with arbitrary names.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(e2e): manually-triggered KWOK closed-loop e2e\n\nAdds an end-to-end test for the KWOK + writable-overlay scenario (#160): capture\na KinD cluster, replay it --writable, run a real `kwok` against the mock server,\ncreate a Pod, and assert the scheduling shim binds it to a node and KWOK drives\nit to Running.\n\n- scripts/e2e-kwok.sh — the scenario (skips gracefully if the `kwok` binary is\n  absent; dies only if kind/kubectl/kshrk are missing).\n- .github/workflows/e2e-kwok.yml — workflow_dispatch (manual) job that installs a\n  pinned `kwok` release, builds kshrk, and runs the test. Not on push/PR because\n  the base runner has no `kwok`.\n- Makefile `e2e-kwok` target; docs/kwok.md \"Validating the loop\" section.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ci(e2e-kwok): verify kwok download checksum, install without elevated perms\n\nAddress Copilot review on #162: the workflow downloaded an unverified binary\nstraight into /usr/local/bin. Now it verifies the download against a pinned\nSHA-256 (a workflow_dispatch input, defaulting to v0.8.0's digest) with\n`sha256sum -c` before making it executable, and installs into $RUNNER_TEMP on\n$GITHUB_PATH rather than a privileged system dir.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(replay-overlay): make synthetic-object creation atomic (stable UID)\n\nAddress Copilot review on #162: synthesizeOverlayObject did a non-atomic\ncheck-then-store (currentObject==nil, then store). Under concurrent pod creates\nwith no captured nodes, two goroutines could both synthesize the same Node with\ndifferent uid/creationTimestamp, mutating an immutable UID and emitting spurious\nwatch events.\n\nAdd overlay.storeIfAbsent — an atomic store-if-no-live-entry under the lock — and\nuse it as synthesizeOverlayObject's final step, so racing creators of the same\nidentity have exactly one winner and a stable UID. A tombstone counts as absent,\nso a deleted synthetic object can still be re-created. This also hardens the\nper-namespace defaults synthesis path.\n\nAdds TestOverlay_StoreIfAbsent_OneWinner (concurrent, run under -race).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(replay-overlay): ship KWOK stages + stamp Pods Pending so the loop works\n\nThe docs/kwok.md walkthrough was wrong: standalone `kwok` loads no lifecycle\nStages on its own and exits with \"no stages found\". Two fixes so the closed loop\nactually runs:\n\n- Ship examples/kwok-stages.yaml — KWOK's own \"fast\" default Stages (node → Ready,\n  Pod → Running, Job Pod → Succeeded, delete), copied verbatim from kwok v0.8.0\n  (Apache-2.0) — and pass it via `kwok --config`. Update docs/kwok.md and\n  scripts/e2e-kwok.sh accordingly.\n- Stamp a freshly created Pod with status.phase=Pending in the overlay, as the\n  apiserver does on create. Without it the pod has no status and KWOK's pod-ready\n  stage (which selects on phase=Pending) never matches.\n\nAdds TestSchedule_CreatedPodIsPending.\n\nPart of #160.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>",
