          "distinct": true,
          "id": "5b0d91adb9ff856728bc466a4ebbac90a5500af9",
          "message": "feat(ui): add global search box to the v2 dashboard (#202)\n\n* feat(ui): add global search box wired to the query engine\n\nPhase 3 of #112 (final phase — JSONPath + full-text/regex query engine and\nCLI landed in #197). Adds a persistent search box to the v2 dashboard's top\nbar, backed by internal/query (the same engine as `kshrk query`):\n\n- internal/ui/v2/search.go: /v2/api/search, dispatching to query.Run\n  (mode=jsonpath, default) or query.SearchText (mode=text/regex), capped at\n  500 results with a truncated flag so a broad pattern against a large\n  archive can't send an unbounded payload to the browser.\n- app.js: a #/search route + renderSearch() results view (rows deep-link to\n  #/object, reusing the existing object-view URL scheme), and a topbar\n  search input + mode select built once (like the replay transport\n  controls) so typing survives unrelated re-renders.\n- docs/web-ui.md: documents the search box and its three modes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ui): make search result rows real links, not div onclick\n\nA <div onclick> result row isn't keyboard-focusable and exposes no link\nsemantics, so tabbing through search results and activating one with Enter\ndidn't work. Use a real <a href> instead (styled to match, text-decoration\nnone / color inherit) — the SPA's hashchange listener already handles the\nnavigation, so this needs no onclick handler at all.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* perf(ui): cap the search response's row slice while building it\n\nserveSearch built a rows slice from every match (duplicating\nresult.Matches, already fully materialized by internal/query) and only\ntruncated afterward. For a broad query on a large capture that's an\nunbounded allocation even though only maxSearchResults rows are ever\nreturned.\n\nTrack total separately (from len(result.Matches), no extra iteration\nneeded) and stop appending to rows once the cap is hit, so peak memory for\nthe response is bounded by maxSearchResults regardless of how many matches\nthe underlying query produced.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>",
